{"Finally, an extraordinary game which I [Golombek] had originally intended
for the “Games Section.” However, I was so bemused and baffled in a vain
attempt to discover the meaning of Black’s opening moves that I decided it
would be better published “in the raw” as it were here. Before playing
through the game readers are advised to weave a circle thrice and close their
eyes in holy dread. In any case it demands attention; for you will not look
upon its like again."} 1. d4 f5 2. Bg5 h6 3. Bh4 h5 4. d5 Nh6 5. Nh3 Nf7 6. e3
d6 7. Be2 g6 8. Nf4 Rg8 9. Ne6 Bxe6 10. dxe6 Ne5 11. Bxh5 Rh8 12. Bxg6+ Nxg6
13. Bg5 c6 14. h4 Qc8 15. h5 Qxe6 16. a3 Ne5 17. f4 Nf7 18. Nd2 ({JS comment:
White's move seems a little too outlandish, even for this game. The score may
be at fault: Brian Denman has suggested a move order change, namely} 18. Kf2 d5
19. Nd2) 18... d5 19. Kf2 Nh6 20. Bxh6 Bxh6 21. Nf3 Nd7 22. Nd4 Qe4 23. g4 fxg4
24. Qxg4 O-O-O 25. Rag1 {(BCM gives 'R-KKt1' and it's not possible to deduce
which rook goes to g1 from subsequent play - JS)} Rdf8 26. Qg6 Nf6 27. Qf5+
Qxf5 28. Nxf5 Ne4+ 29. Ke2 Rxf5 30. Rg6 Nf6 {Source: BCM, May 1959, p137} 0-1

